[s0l0!-header][h1]Goroutines[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Let's have a look at the following program:[code format="go" codeId="3359"]func out(from, to int) {
  for i:=from; i<=to; i++ {
    time.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)
    fmt.Println(i)
  }
}
func main() {
  out(0, 5)
  out(6, 10)
}[/code]
The [b]out()[/b] function simply outputs numbers in the given range. We use a [b]time.Sleep()[/b] to emulate work being done between the outputs just for demonstration purposes. It simply waits for the provided time ([b]50ms[/b]) before continuing the execution.

Now, if we call the function in main two times, the first call will execute first followed by the second call.
This will generate the output of 0 to 5, then 6 to 10.
[note]This is called a sequential program, as the statements are executed one after the other. The first call needs to complete, before the second call starts.[/note]

